<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/expert/solid-state-physics">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This expert lesson descends beneath the syllabus you built in Circuits and Ohms Law,
    Magnetism and Induction and Electrostatics to the quantum lattice that produces it —
    with Quantum Foundations supplying the wave mechanics and Thermal Physics the energy
    scale (room-temperature kT is about 0.025 eV, a number that decides everything here).
    Every rule you learned at circuit level turns out to be a macroscopic average over
    crystal-level behaviour; this lesson computes those averages.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain ferromagnetism, paramagnetism and diamagnetism from electron spins, domains and the Curie point;</li>
    <li>compute drift speeds from I = nAqv and replace the "fast electrons" folk model with the field picture;</li>
    <li>derive resistance as scattering — and explain what makes superconductivity shocking;</li>
    <li>use band gaps to predict insulator breakdown, LED colours and solar-cell response;</li>
    <li>read the Hall effect, induction cooktops and transformer cores as solids engineering their own electromagnetism.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Solid-state physics explains matter you can hold: why copper conducts, silicon
    semi-conducts, diamond doesn't, and magnets magnetise. The key idea is
    <strong>band structure</strong>: in a crystal, individual atomic energy levels smear
    into bands, and everything follows from whether the highest occupied band is full,
    empty, or half-full. Read against the electricity-and-magnetism syllabus, the expert
    move is consistent: every school-level rule (magnetism, resistance, circuit laws,
    safety limits, induction) is a macroscopic average over quantum behaviour in the
    solid, and solid-state physics is the science of computing that average. The syllabus's
    five topics become five places where the quantum lattice shows through.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="opposite temperature responses — the crossing is why the same crystal can be tuned either way" widget="plotter" config={bandCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> two curves with opposite slopes. The grey metal curve
    falls with temperature — lattice vibrations multiply and scatter the carriers; the red
    semiconductor curve rises steeply — heat promotes electrons across the band gap faster
    than scattering can hinder them. Watch the crossing point: left of it the semiconductor
    is nearly an insulator, right of it it becomes a decent conductor, and the same crystal
    can be parked anywhere in between by doping and temperature control. Nearly every
    semiconductor device, from thermistors to processors, is management of the balance
    these two curves encode.
  </p>

  <p>
    Metals conduct <em>worse</em> when heated (lattice vibrations scatter electrons);
    semiconductors conduct <em>better</em> (heat promotes electrons across the band gap).
    The crossing in the normalised plot marks why doping and temperature control are the
    whole art of semiconductor engineering.
  </p>

  <h2>Magnetism in matter: spins, domains and the Curie point</h2>
  <p>
    School magnetism (4.1) classifies materials: iron sticks to magnets, copper doesn't.
    The quantum story: magnetism comes from unpaired electron <em>spins</em>. In
    ferromagnets (iron, cobalt, nickel) neighbouring spins align cooperatively into
    <strong>domains</strong> — regions of uniform alignment, typically micrometres across.
    An unmagnetised bar has randomly oriented domains; magnetising it is domain-realigning,
    which is why hammering or heating a magnet can demagnetise it. Heating destroys the
    alignment entirely at the <strong>Curie temperature</strong> — 770 °C for iron — above
    which ferromagnetism vanishes and the material is merely paramagnetic; the same
    lattice, the same atoms, and the magnetism is gone, because the alignment was a
    collective phase, not a property of individual atoms. Hard drives exploit hysteresis:
    the domain walls resist small fields, so written magnetisation persists — memory as
    metastability. Diamagnetism (everything repels a field, weakly) and paramagnetism
    (unpaired spins align weakly with a field) complete the classification; the refrigerator
    magnet on your fridge is a ferromagnet engineered with fine particles to hold its
    domains against both time and temptation.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "magnets attract metals." Magnets attract
    <em>ferromagnetic</em> metals — iron, nickel, cobalt (and some alloys). Copper,
    aluminium, gold, silver, lead and zinc are not attracted, and are weakly
    <em>diamagnetic</em> or paramagnetic instead: the fridge magnet ignores the aluminium
    can and grips the steel lid. The electron configuration decides — unpaired inner
    electrons plus the right lattice spacing — which is why stainless steel is often
    barely magnetic (its chromium and nickel content alter the lattice) and why
    "magnetic" is a terrible scrap-metal test for brass.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> magnetism in matter is collective — domains, alignment and a
    Curie point where the collective phase simply switches off. Next, the same microscopic
    descent for electricity: what actually drifts through a wire, and how slowly.
  </p>

  <h2>Electrical quantities through a crystal: drift, density, speed</h2>
  <p>
    The electrical quantities (4.2) — charge, current, voltage, resistance — acquire
    microscopic meaning in a solid. Current is charge in transit: I = nAqv, with n the
    mobile charge density, A the wire's cross-section, q the carrier charge, v the drift
    speed. Run the numbers for 1 A in 1 mm² copper (n ≈ 8.5 × 10²⁸ carriers per m³, one
    free electron per atom): v = 1 ÷ (8.5 × 10²⁸ × 10⁻⁶ × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹) ≈ 7 × 10⁻⁵ m/s —
    under a tenth of a millimetre per second. The light comes on instantly not because
    electrons sprint round the circuit but because the <em>electric field</em> establishes
    itself at near light speed, setting the whole wire's carriers drifting at once. That
    image — enormous charge density, glacial drift speed, instant field — is the correct
    replacement for the "electrons whiz through wires" folk model, and it is why electrical
    signals, plumbing analogies and water in pipes eventually all fail as models.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "electrons carry the energy by travelling fast through
    the wire." The drift speed above is a slow crawl; the energy travels in the
    electromagnetic field <em>around</em> the wire, at close to light speed. The electrons
    are the conveyor belt, not the cargo — and in an alternating-current circuit they
    simply oscillate in place, reversing direction 100 times a second at 50 Hz mains
    without ever completing a trip from socket to lamp.
  </p>

  <h2>Circuits and resistance: scattering, not rubbing</h2>
  <p>
    Circuit behaviour (4.3) gets its microscopic foundation: resistance is
    <em>scattering</em>. Electrons in a perfect periodic lattice would glide unimpeded
    (quantum mechanics lets waves pass through perfect crystals); resistance comes from
    imperfections — vibrating atoms (thermal), impurity atoms, grain boundaries. That is
    why resistivity rises with temperature in metals, why alloys (deliberate disorder) resist
    more than pure metals, and why a copper wire's resistance is computable: R = ρL/A, with
    copper's ρ ≈ 1.7 × 10⁻⁸ Ω·m. Worked: 10 m of 1 mm² copper has R = 1.7 × 10⁻⁸ × 10 ÷
    10⁻⁶ = 0.17 Ω — at 10 A it dissipates I²R = 17 W, which is why extension leads have
    minimum thickness ratings and why the series-wiring of a whole house through one thin
    cable would be an incendiary device. The same scattering physics explains
    superconductivity's shock: below a critical temperature, scattering mechanisms die and
    resistance falls to <em>exactly</em> zero (not merely small) — persistent currents in
    superconducting rings have run for decades without decay.
  </p>

  <h2>Safety, limits and breakdown: when solids fail</h2>
  <p>
    Safety (4.4) in solid-state terms is the study of how solids fail under electrical
    stress. Insulators resist because their electrons have no accessible states — but every
    insulator has a <strong>breakdown field</strong>: force enough voltage across, and
    electrons are torn free, avalanche multiplies them, and the solid becomes a
    conductor in a flash. Air sparks over at about 3 million volts per metre — which is
    why 11,000 V distribution lines need their air gaps and why a fly landing across a
    switch's terminals is fine but a wet finger is not. Fuses encode the same physics in
    metal: the fuse element is sized so that double the rated current melts it (I²R
    heating again), interrupting the circuit before the house wiring — with its own
    thermal limits — cooks. Semiconductor devices add their own safety physics: thermal
    runaway (a hot transistor conducts more, heats more, conducts more) is why amplifiers
    need heat sinks, and why battery packs carry both temperature sensors and current
    interrupters. The syllabus's "never exceed the fuse rating" is band structure, drift
    and scattering, written as house rules.
  </p>
  <p>
    The LED is band engineering's most visible product, and its arithmetic is worth
    doing once. An electron falling across a band gap of energy E emits a photon of
    wavelength λ = hc/E — the rule of thumb is 1,240 nm per eV, so a 2.7 eV gap
    gives blue near 460 nm, a 2.3 eV gap green, and a 1.9 eV gap red. The historical
    problem was the blue end: the wide-gap crystals would not grow cleanly, and it
    took Akasaki, Amano and Nakamura's 1990s gallium-nitride breakthrough (Nobel
    2014) to complete the primary colours — white LEDs are blue emitters coated in
    a yellow phosphor. The same equation backwards runs solar cells (photons above
    the gap create carriers; below it they pass through) and laser diodes, which is
    why "band gap engineering" is a job title.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "insulators never conduct electricity." Insulators
    conduct <em>nothing</em> only up to their breakdown field; past it, they conduct
    catastrophically. Air is an excellent insulator until about 3 kV per millimetre of gap
    — then lightning. Glass, rubber and plastics all have breakdown ratings, and every
    high-voltage design is a negotiation with them (insulator discs on pylons exist to
    lengthen the leakage path, not because the ceramic is special). "Insulator" means
    "withstands", never "impossible" — the band gap delays conduction, it does not forbid
    it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the solid's electronic jobs are now all visible at once —
    carriers that drift, scatter, avalanche past breakdown, and jump band gaps that emit
    exactly the photon the gap size dictates. The final section turns the solid from a
    component into an actor: materials whose own electromagnetic responses build the
    machines.
  </p>

  <h2>Electromagnetic effects in solids: induction, Hall and machines</h2>
  <p>
    Electromagnetic effects (4.5) find their deepest engineering expression in solids. The
    <strong>Hall effect</strong> (1879): run current along a strip in a magnetic field and
    a voltage appears <em>across</em> the strip — the magnetic force pushes carriers to one
    edge until the accumulated charge's electric field balances it. The sign of the Hall
    voltage revealed, before quantum theory existed, that some materials carry current on
    positive carriers (the "holes" of semiconductors) — a puzzle whose resolution is pure
    band theory. Hall sensors now measure position in every phone and car wheel (ABS).
    Induction cooktops drive eddy currents in the pan's steel base — I²R heating with the
    hob's glass staying cool. Motors, generators and transformers (the school syllabus's
    machines) all lean on the solid's magnetic response: soft iron cores (domains that
    flip easily, low hysteresis loss) versus hard magnetic steels (permanent magnets) are
    the same phenomenon engineered in opposite directions — and transformer laminations
    exist to chop the eddy-current loops that solid cores would offer.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why does the resistivity of a metal rise with temperature, while that of a semiconductor falls? Answer with the two mechanisms.</li>
    <li>An LED's crystal has a band gap of 2.7 eV. Using the rule of thumb 1,240 nm per eV, estimate the emission wavelength — and say why the blue LED was the hard one to build historically.</li>
    <li>The Hall effect revealed something about charge carriers before quantum theory existed. What was it, and why did the sign of the voltage matter?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) In a metal, heating multiplies lattice vibrations, so
    carriers scatter more often and drift is impeded — resistance rises. In a semiconductor,
    heat promotes electrons across the narrow band gap, and the explosion in carrier density
    outpaces the extra scattering — resistance falls. (2) λ = hc/E gives 1,240 ÷ 2.7 ≈ 460
    nm — blue. The blue end was hard because the wide-gap crystals (gallium nitride) would
    not grow cleanly until Akasaki, Amano and Nakamura's 1990s breakthrough; without blue,
    there was no phosphor-coated white LED and no completed primary set. (3) The Hall
    voltage appears across the strip because the magnetic force pushes carriers to one edge;
    its <em>sign</em> revealed that some materials carry current on positive carriers — the
    holes of semiconductors — a puzzle whose resolution is pure band theory, and which is
    why Hall sensors now sit in every phone and car wheel.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Band structure decides everything: full, empty or half-full highest band makes an insulator, semiconductor or metal.</li>
    <li>Ferromagnetism is collective spin alignment in domains, erased at the Curie point — a phase, not a per-atom property.</li>
    <li>Current is enormous charge density drifting glacially; the energy travels in the field around the wire at near light speed.</li>
    <li>Resistance is scattering: vibrating atoms, impurities and grain boundaries — and zero scattering is superconductivity.</li>
    <li>Every insulator has a breakdown field; "insulator" means withstands, never impossible.</li>
    <li>Band gap arithmetic sets LED colours (1,240 nm per eV) and solar-cell response; doping retunes the crystal at parts per million.</li>
    <li>The Hall effect, soft and hard magnetic steels and laminated cores are solids engineering their own electromagnetism.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Quantum Field Theory, the sibling expert lesson, generalises the quantum story you have
    been reading — fields whose excitations are particles — to which the phonons and Cooper
    pairs above are a warm-up. Electrodynamics (Maxwell) is the companion expert
    lesson whose ferromagnetism aside points here, and whose field picture this lesson
    finally grounds in matter.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Bands from atoms.</strong> Bring N atoms together and each atomic level splits
    into N closely spaced levels — for N ~ 10²³, a continuum: a <strong>band</strong>.
    Between bands lie gaps with no states at all. Everything follows: a metal's Fermi level
    sits inside a band (states available at zero cost); an insulator's sits in a wide gap
    (diamond's is 5.5 eV, and thermal energy at room temperature is about 0.025 eV — a
    hopeless climb); a semiconductor's gap is narrow (silicon's 1.1 eV), so a few electrons
    make it across, each leaving a mobile hole. Doping sharpens the game: phosphorus in
    silicon donates one extra electron (n-type); boron leaves a hole (p-type) — at
    concentrations around one impurity atom in a million, and the crystal barely notices
    it is now a different electronic material. The p–n junction is the band theory's
    masterpiece: electrons diffuse across, set up a depletion field, and the junction
    conducts one way — a diode — and with engineered bias, a transistor: the 1947 Bell
    Labs invention (Bardeen, Brattain, Shockley, Nobel 1956) that made information a
    physical industry.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Phonons and the quantum solid.</strong> The lattice is not a rigid backdrop;
    its vibrations are quantised as <strong>phonons</strong>, and they run the thermal
    physics: heat capacity (Einstein and Debye's quantum fix of the classical
    Dulong–Petit puzzle), thermal conductivity (diamond conducts heat ~2,000 W/(m·K),
    five times copper, via stiff covalent bonds and fast phonons), and the electron
    scattering that creates resistance. Phonons also mediate superconductivity: BCS theory
    (Bardeen, Cooper, Schrieffer, Nobel 1972) shows electrons pair through lattice
    distortion into bosonic Cooper pairs that condense into a state with zero resistance
    and the Meissner effect (magnetic field expulsion — levitating magnets are a party
    trick of thermodynamics). The high-temperature superconductors found in 1986 (Nobel
    1987) still lack a settled theory — one of physics' longest-running open problems —
    and room-temperature superconductivity under extreme pressure (claimed, contested,
    retracted in part) keeps the field honest.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Engineered matter.</strong> Modern solid-state physics is less about finding
    materials than designing them: band gaps tuned by composition (gallium arsenide,
    gallium nitride — the blue LED, Nobel 2014, needed crystals nobody could grow until
    the 1990s), quantum wells and heterostructures grown one atomic layer at a time by
    molecular beam epitaxy, and two-dimensional materials (graphene, isolated 2004, Nobel
    2010) whose carriers behave as massless particles. Every chip is a solid-state physics
    exam with ten billion questions; every LED is a band gap chosen for its colour. The
    school syllabus's copper wire and iron magnet are where the subject started; the
    subject now ends — for now — in deliberate atomic architecture.
  </p>
